WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.462
If you ask a roboticist,
somebody who studies robots for a living,

00:00:04.462 --> 00:00:05.505
what is a robot?

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:09.634
It's actually a very challenging question
to answer.

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:14.055
My name is Rebecca Kramer-Bottiglio, and I'm a
mechanical engineer and materials scientist.

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:18.435
When you conjure up an image of a robot,
you typically think of rigid machines

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:21.938
that perform repeated tasks,
and they don't adapt

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:25.859
to their environment because that's not
what they've been designed to do.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:31.197
But when we think of robots for the future,
we imagine things like animals, like ourselves

00:00:31.197 --> 00:00:33.408
We are incredibly adaptable.

00:00:33.408 --> 00:00:39.414
We adapt our body configuration and our behavior
toward different tasks and environments

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:42.709
and we want to design
robots that can do the same.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:45.295
And when we look at how animals adapt 

00:00:45.295 --> 00:00:48.298
to different tasks and environments, what we notice is that

00:00:48.298 --> 00:00:51.051
all animals are at least partially soft.

00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:54.262
There are no known animals in the world
that are completely rigid,

00:00:54.262 --> 00:00:57.807
which is in contrast to the way
that we typically design robots.

00:00:59.684 --> 00:01:04.689
And so I study soft robots, which are robots
made out of soft  materials.

00:01:04.689 --> 00:01:09.652
Soft robotics is a broad field
touching on wearables,

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:13.907
and manipulation, shapeshifting
robots, and locomotion robots.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:18.203
My group is developing novel, responsive
and intelligent materials

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:23.416
and embedding them into robots that can
perform novel behaviors like shape change.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:27.170
And we draw upon inspiration from biology.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.591
We have a turtle and tortoise inspired
robot platform with morphing limbs.

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:35.011
Turtles and tortoises
are morphologically very similar,

00:01:35.011 --> 00:01:36.805
but they differ primarily in their limbs.

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:40.433
So when you think of sea turtles,
you think of elongated flippers

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.769
that are relatively flexible
and flat.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:48.483
Tortoises have rounded limbs that are better for load
bearing and terrestrial locomotion.

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.402
So what we've developed is a morphing limb platform

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:56.783
that can switch back and forth between a flipper-like
morphology and a leg-like morphology.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:02:03.456
And it allows the robot to accomplish
efficient locomotion both in water and on land.

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:19.013
What I love about working in robotics
is all of the intellectual challenges.

00:02:19.013 --> 00:02:24.561
We have the opportunity to redefine
what is a robot from the ground up.

